t  least it was pretty it was a pretty  jarring shift because it's like it's  like the first two accidents movie feel  like they would have been right at home  with the first two maybe three Harry  Potter movies you know with that lighter  feel oh you know we're going to school  and in this case you know we're  collecting the creatures and then they  get the last creature and all of a  sudden the third act feels like it's out  of one of the last movies in the Harry  Potter series you know where  everything's dark and dour and all big  huge stakes and I'm it's there's a  reason that the Harry Potter films took  eight movies to get from that whimsical  starting place to that dark ending place  this tries to do it all in one film and  the transition doesn't work now I will  grant that part of the problem may have  been just my experience going to watch  it and by that I mean I had to see it in  3d it was the the time that I was  available to see it it was a 3d showing  and unfortunately at the time I got  there got stuck and lousy seating sort  of way off to the side sort of cocked it  a weird angle which is not a good angle  to view a movie at all it's certainly  not a good angle to view a 3d movie at a  movie like this you really shouldn't see  in 3d because especially towards the end  this is it's very dark lighting that  never works well in 3d so I had kind of  a bad visual experience watching this  thing and it is possible that if I'd had  better seating for that third act that  it would have worked better I tend to  not think so though because my problems  are all your structure and tone and  story I didn't I had issues with my  problems weren't that I was visually  bored the fact that I couldn't tell what  was going on was a problem but it wasn't  the overriding one I mean unless the  visuals were so spectacular that it just  overrode the part of my brain that was  picking apart the story then I probably  would still have these same issues and  cuz that whole en